VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: GPO-004
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe panic attack and health anxiety regarding breast cancer risk following a sudden family tragedy this evening.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, adult daughter
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an acutely distressed female experiencing severe tachycardia, hyperventilation, and extreme psychiatric distress following news of a first-degree relative's sudden death from metastatic breast cancer tonight. Urgent face-to-face assessment was required to provide definitive clinical reassurance via physical examination, arrest the acute panic, and arrange an immediate BreastScreen referral pathway. Delaying assessment would have resulted in an acute psychiatric crisis and an unnecessary overnight ED presentation for panic.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: "My sister just died from breast cancer tonight, I'm terrified I have it too, and I cannot breathe or sleep until a doctor checks me."
History of Present Illness: The patient, an asymptomatic female typically aged between 50 and 74 years old, experienced a sudden and severe onset of health anxiety three hours ago. There is a strong personal or family history of breast or ovarian cancer impacting risk assessment, which was abruptly brought to the forefront tonight. Despite the acute psychiatric distress, the patient confirms an absence of new palpable breast lumps, skin dimpling, or nipple retraction, and an absence of spontaneous pathological nipple discharge. The extreme anxiety resulted in a sympathetic nervous system surge, preventing sleep and causing somatic symptoms of panic.
Functional Status: Independent, currently experiencing acute situational distress.
Social/Safety Concerns: High acute anxiety requiring strong familial support; daughter is present to assist with de-escalation.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 150/92 | HR: 112 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Visibly anxious, crying intermittently, easily startled, rapid speech.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Tachycardic, regular rhythm, no murmurs. Respiratory: Clear air entry bilaterally, mild hyperventilation. Examination confirms the patient is an asymptomatic female 50-74 years with a complete absence of palpable lumps or discharge. A comprehensive discussion of screening benefits and limitations was conducted to ground the patient. Documentation of previous mammogram dates and results was secured. Expected investigation results include bilateral cranio-caudal and mediolateral oblique mammographic views demonstrating no suspicious microcalcifications or spiculated masses.
Environmental Observations: Clean, safe environment; strong family support present.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications; verified.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: BreastScreen Referral and Counselling (Secondary to Acute Health Anxiety) - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Acute Mastitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal temperature, absence of focal erythema, tenderness, or induration on clinical examination.

Breast Malignancy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of palpable masses, skin tethering, axillary lymphadenopathy, or pathological discharge, rendering the patient clinically asymptomatic.

Acute Coronary Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Tachycardia linked directly to emotional distress, absence of chest pain, and normal oxygen saturation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient presented with acute, severe autonomic hyperarousal and panic directly resulting from catastrophic news regarding a first-degree relative's breast cancer. Telephone triage correctly recognized that the intense fear of undiagnosed malignancy was causing a severe acute stress reaction. Urgent face-to-face assessment was necessary to perform a thorough clinical examination, rule out actual physical pathology, and provide undeniable clinical reassurance to arrest the panic attack. Issuing a documented BreastScreen referral and engaging in comprehensive counselling provided immediate psychological relief. Delaying this intervention would have led to an unwarranted ED presentation for acute panic overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Conducted a thorough, reassuring physical examination. Completed a formal BreastScreen Referral and engaged in extensive reassurance and counselling regarding modern mammography and false-positive potentials.
Positive Behavioral Change: To channel the acute health anxiety into productive action, commit to performing regular breast self-awareness checks exclusively on the first day of each month. Pair this structured routine with marking a calendar. Restricting the checking behavior to a scheduled time prevents obsessive, panic-driven palpation, actively reducing anxiety through structured, predictable self-care.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 7 days to follow up on the BreastScreen appointment and monitor anxiety levels.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Present to the nearest Emergency Department or call 000 if your panic symptoms escalate to sudden, severe, crushing chest pain, or if you become entirely unable to catch your breath despite reassurance.

